```
if (!mbox || !mbox->dev || !mbox->ops || !mbox->num_chans)
    return -EINVAL;
...
struct mbox_chan *chan = &mbox->chans[i];
```
Reason:
The original sanity check omitted `!mbox->chans`, so if a mailbox controller registered with `chans` set to `NULL`, the condition passed. The subsequent loop dereferences `mbox->chans[i]`, which causes a null pointer dereference (OOPS) because `mbox->chans` is `NULL`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (!mbox || !mbox->dev || !mbox->ops || !mbox->chans || !mbox->num_chans)
    return -EINVAL;
```
Reason:
Adding `!mbox->chans` to the condition ensures that the function returns `-EINVAL` early if the channel array pointer is `NULL`. This prevents the later loop from dereferencing a null pointer, providing a graceful failure instead of an OOPS.

